To test if was a `ParticleProcessMaterial` related issue, I used as `Process Material` a very simple custom `ShaderMaterial` with the following code:

```.cpp
shader_type particles;

void start() {
    VELOCITY = vec3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0); // Initial velocity
}

void process() {
    VELOCITY.y += 1.0; // Apply gravity
}
```

...same problem, **no particles are showed**, so I think we have to search in another direction. 

**PS:** of course I tested it also on **NOT Adreno 3XX** devices, and works fine.

@clayjohn from my research, it seems that the problem is that the [drivers/gles3/shaders/particles_copy.glsl](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/master/drivers/gles3/shaders/particles_copy.glsl) shader, receives incorrect parameters (specifically **`velocity_flags`**), in fact by commenting on the following line, the particles appear even if they do not move.

```.cpp
// if (bool(floatBitsToUint(velocity_flags.w) & PARTICLE_FLAG_ACTIVE))

```

I tried to figure out who passed the parameters to [particles_copy.glsl](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/master/drivers/gles3/shaders/particles_copy.glsl) shader, but I couldn't.

I thought it was passed to it by the [particles.glsl](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/master/drivers/gles3/shaders/particles.glsl), but by changing its **`out_`** parameters, [particles_copy.glsl](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/master/drivers/gles3/shaders/particles_copy.glsl) still gets the wrong ones.
